# rule.forbid.implicit-assumptions

never let correctness rest on a condition no one enforces and no one wrote down. an assumption = a claim that the world keeps a condition we cannot make hold; we enforce it by assertion (a surfer's birth date is true · stripe delivers each webhook at least once · the clock never goes backward)
smell: code correct only if an upstream, a third party, a clock, or a human behaves — with no line that says so · "that never happens" in a review · a third-party response used with no parse
cure: (1) convert it where you can — parse at the boundary, and the shape becomes an invariant you own · (2) cap the blast radius so a break is benign (an idempotent consumer absorbs a duplicate webhook) · (3) assert it: detect each break and fail fast (an absent webhook surfaces within a day) · (4) declare it in `inventory.of=assumption` with its evidence, strength, blast radius, required response on each break (absorbed or surfaced), and review-by date — declarative, never the mechanism
enforcement: correctness that rests on an implicit assumption = blocker; a break with no required response = blocker
full: rule.require.assumptions-with-evidence · precedent: rely/guarantee (jones 1983), assume/guarantee (misra & chandy 1981)
